From 2cdac5742c6f6c1fa90b5f909f07ce03144c7098 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:53:07 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] wisp: split writes into packets of 64 KiB, each within flow
 control

A stream write went out as one DATA packet whatever its size: an http
request body was one message of the whole body (hyper hands the socket
the head and body in one buffer), and a wisp server that bounds its
WebSocket messages closes the whole connection past the bound, every
stream on it. The holyWisp relays take 262149 bytes (256 KiB of payload
plus the header) and close with 1009, so one 300 KB upload to a plain
http site killed every request and socket the page had open. HTTPS was
spared only because each TLS record goes out as its own packet.

Writes now go out as DATA packets of at most 64 KiB (MAX_DATA_PAYLOAD);
no extension advertises a server's bound, and 64 KiB is far under any
server's. AsyncWrite::poll_write sends one packet per call and returns
what it took (hyper, rustls and write_all send the rest), and the Sink
sends a large item as several packets. Each packet waits for flow
control credit: the AsyncWrite side that every TCP stream uses never
looked at CONTINUE and sent past the server's buffer. While a stream
waits it flushes the transport, since a packet handed to it may only go
out on its next poll and the server sends no CONTINUE before it has
counted the window's last packet (an upload of 20 MiB through holyWisp
stopped one packet short of its window without that).

A UDP datagram cannot be split. One over the limit (it is above the
65507 bytes an IPv4 datagram can carry) fails that send alone with
DatagramTooLarge, as EMSGSIZE does on a native socket, and the stream
stays open. A UDP stream from connect_udp gets send(datagram) for that,
next to its read and write streams: a WritableStream errors as a whole
when one write fails, so its write rejects with the same error.
